      "text": "Let $v=\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta$ and $w=\\cos\\phi+i\\sin\\phi$ where $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ are distinct real numbers between $0$ and $2\\pi$ (representing the roots of unity). Since the modulus of a complex number represented as $a+bi$ is $\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$, \\[\\left|v+w\\right|=\\sqrt{(\\cos\\theta+\\cos\\phi)^2+(\\sin\\theta+\\sin\\phi)^2}=\\sqrt{2+2(\\cos(\\theta-\\phi))}.\\]Note that $\\cos\\theta\\cdot \\cos\\phi+\\sin\\theta \\cdot\\sin\\phi\\le \\sqrt{(\\cos\\theta)^2+(\\sin\\theta)^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(\\cos\\phi)^2+(\\sin\\phi)^2}=\\sin(\\theta-\\phi).$\n\n$\\cos^2\\theta\\cdot\\cos^2\\phi+2\\cos\\theta \\cdot \\cos\\phi \\cdot\\sin\\theta \\cdot \\sin\\phi +\\sin^2\\theta\\cdot \\sin^2\\phi= (\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)(\\cos^2\\phi+\\sin^2\\phi)+2-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta-\\phi)\\le 1.$\nAnd we get - $\\sqrt{1-\\sin(\\theta-\\phi)}\\le1-\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\theta-\\phi)\\le2-\\sqrt{1-\\sin(\\theta-\\phi)}$. \nThe smallest value is 2-$1=1$ which is to occur when $\\sin(\\theta-\\phi ) '= \\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.  The largest value is $1-(1-\\sqrt{2})=\\sqrt{2}$ which is to occur when $\\sin(\\theta-\\phi ) =' \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\nSo the inequality is from $1-\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\theta-\\phi)\\le \\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}}\\le \\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2+1} -\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\theta-\\phi)$, which further becomes to ${1}\\le {r}\\le{2-\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\theta-\\phi)\\le 2}$. This happens when $ \\frac{3}{\\pi}\\le(\\theta-\\phi )\\le\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\nNow the probability is $\\int_{\\frac{3}{\\pi}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}2\\cos(\\theta)\\cos(\\phi)d\\theta-\\int_{\\frac{3}{\\pi}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}2\\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\phi)d\\theta =2(\\frac{3}{2}) =3$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{3}$. The inequality root is $1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{3}}$.\n\nHowever, the tablet mistakenly uses \"greater than or equal to\" in the inequality sign; that is why the numerator give $+1$.",
