      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we'll first determine the conditions under which \\(2 + \\sqrt{3} \\le |v + w|\\). We'll start with the first part of the problem, choosing two distinct roots \\(v\\) and \\(w\\) of the equation \\(z^{1997} - 1 = 0\\).\n\nWe can certainly take \\(4\\) (which is the 4th root of unity) as a root to proceed. Therefore, we investigate the expression for \\(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\), which in terms of De Moivre's formula represents twice (in the length), plus the magnitude of the scaling factor of \\(3^{1/2}\\) which represents the distance from the origin. \n\nNow we have that \\(v^{1997} = 1\\) and \\(w^{1997} = 1\\) implying that \\(v = \\xi\\), and \\(w = \\xi^t\\), where \\(t = 1997k + 1\\). Then, the magnitude \\(|(v + w)/2|\\) falls on the unit real circle into which \\(v + w\\in \\mathbb{C}\\) by the triangle inequality, proved to be a complex number of mod \\(1\\).\n\nBecause one term of \\(v^{1997} = 1\\) and \\(w^{1997} = 1\\), and considering the property of complex roots of unity evenly distributed over the unit circle, we see that \\(|v^{1997}| = |w^{1997}| = 1\\). Therefore, \\(|v + w| = 2 \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi m}{1997}\\right) + \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi n}{1997}\\right)\\). \n\nBecause \\(1997\\) is odd, we can conclude \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) must be unequal only for specific values of \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) that split numbers modularly 1997 into \\(1\\) and some \\(q\\). Therefore, \\(cot^2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{1997}\\right) \\le |v + w|\\), and the condition for \\(2 + \\sqrt{3} \\le |v + w|\\) leads to \\(|cot^{-1}(\\sqrt{3})|\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), or:\n\n\\[\n2 . \\left|\\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{3})\\right| \\le |cot^{-1}(\\sqrt{3})|.\n\\]\n\nWe know \\(\\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\), and consequently \\(2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\le|cot^{-1}(\\sqrt{3})|). Hence, the segment where probability applies is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\le \\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{3}) \\le \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe length of this segment is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3} - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). Dividing by the circumference of the circle implies we have a probability of \\(\\frac{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}{2\\pi} = \\frac{1}{6}\\) that the sqrt inequality for \\(|v + w|\\) ends up satisfying. Hence, the probability that \\(\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}} \\le |v + w|\\) is equal to \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
